# Hardware Assessment

## Overview

Before selecting the operating system, I perform a complete hardware assessment to evaluate the device's overall condition, identify performance limitations, and determine whether hardware upgrades are recommended.

Every computer should be evaluated as a complete system rather than focusing on individual components.

---

# Objectives

- Evaluate overall hardware performance.
- Identify hardware limitations.
- Recommend suitable hardware upgrades.
- Select the most appropriate Windows version.
- Improve long-term stability and user experience.

---

# Processor (CPU)

Evaluate:

- Manufacturer (Intel / AMD)
- Processor family
- Generation
- Model
- Base and Turbo clock speed
- Number of cores and threads
- Processor class (U, H, HQ, HX, etc.)

The processor is one of the primary factors when selecting an operating system.

Older processors generally provide a better experience with Windows 10, while modern processors are well suited for Windows 11.

Always evaluate the processor generation together with its architecture and intended workload.

---

# Memory (RAM)

Check:

- Installed capacity
- Memory type (DDR3 / DDR4 / DDR5)
- Memory speed
- Available upgrade slots
- Maximum supported capacity

Increasing RAM may improve multitasking performance and reduce reliance on the Windows page file.

Recommend RAM upgrades only when they provide a noticeable improvement in real-world performance.

---

# Graphics (GPU)

Determine whether the system uses:

- Integrated Graphics
- Dedicated Graphics

Evaluate the GPU according to the customer's workload.

Integrated graphics are generally suitable for:

- Office work
- Internet browsing
- Education
- General home use

Dedicated graphics are recommended for:

- Gaming
- Engineering
- 3D Design
- Video Editing

While Windows 11 does not require a dedicated graphics card, a stronger GPU can improve the overall visual experience on modern systems.

---

# Storage

Evaluate:

- HDD
- SATA SSD
- NVMe SSD

Check:

- Storage type
- Capacity
- Available free space
- Future expansion options

Whenever possible, recommend upgrading from HDD to SSD.

Larger storage capacity provides greater flexibility for future software installations and user data.

Cloud storage can also be recommended as an additional backup solution.

---

# Cooling System

Inspect:

- Fan condition
- Dust accumulation
- Airflow
- Thermal paste condition
- Operating temperatures

A stable cooling system improves performance, reliability, and hardware lifespan.

---

# TPM Assessment

Verify whether the system supports Trusted Platform Module (TPM).

Check:

- TPM version
- TPM status (Enabled / Disabled)
- BIOS support

Windows 11 officially requires TPM 2.0.

Do not assume TPM support based only on the manufacturing year.

Always verify the TPM version before selecting Windows 11.

If TPM 2.0 is unavailable but the hardware is otherwise capable, this information will be used later when preparing the installation media.

---

# Driver Availability

Before selecting Windows, verify that compatible drivers are available from the manufacturer's official website.

Older devices may require compatibility mode or legacy drivers.

---

# Upgrade Potential

Evaluate whether the device supports future upgrades.

Examples:

- SSD upgrade
- RAM upgrade
- Additional storage
- CPU upgrade (Desktop systems)

Recommend upgrades only when they provide meaningful performance improvements.

Avoid unnecessary expenses for the customer.

---

# Customer Usage

Understand how the customer intends to use the computer.

Examples:

- Office Work
- Education
- Programming
- Engineering
- Graphic Design
- Video Editing
- Gaming
- General Home Use

The intended workload has a direct impact on hardware recommendations and Windows selection.

---

# Compatibility Assessment

After evaluating the hardware, summarize the device's overall condition.

| Component | Status | Notes |
|-----------|--------|-------|
| CPU | Good | Suitable for Windows 10 |
| RAM | Good | Enough for everyday use |
| GPU | Acceptable | Integrated graphics |
| Storage | Excellent | SSD installed |
| Cooling | Good | No overheating detected |
| TPM | Supported / Not Supported | Verify version before Windows 11 |

---

# Upgrade Priority

When recommending upgrades, I generally follow this priority:

1. Replace HDD with SSD
2. Clean the cooling system
3. Replace thermal paste
4. Upgrade RAM (when beneficial)
5. Increase storage capacity

The goal is to achieve the greatest improvement with the lowest reasonable cost.

---

# Cost vs Performance

I recommend hardware upgrades only when they provide a noticeable improvement in daily use.

I avoid recommending unnecessary upgrades that increase costs without offering meaningful benefits.

---

# Reliability

My priority is system stability rather than achieving the highest benchmark scores.

A stable, responsive computer is more valuable than a faster system that suffers from overheating or instability.

---

# Technician Experience

Based on my practical experience:

- Windows 10 Pro remains an excellent choice for many older systems.
- I prefer clean installations instead of in-place upgrades whenever possible.
- SSD upgrades usually provide the largest performance improvement.
- Every device should be evaluated individually.
- Hardware specifications should always be considered together, not separately.

---

# Real-World Example

**Device**

- Dell Latitude
- Intel Core i3-4005U
- 12 GB DDR3 RAM
- 240 GB SSD
- Intel Integrated Graphics

**Recommendation**

Windows 10 Pro (22H2)

**Reason**

Provides a smoother and more stable experience than Windows 11 while maintaining excellent compatibility with the hardware.

---

# Final Assessment

Before proceeding with Windows installation, determine:

- Is the hardware suitable?
- Are upgrades recommended?
- Which Windows version is the best choice?
- Are there any hardware limitations that should be explained to the customer?

Document all recommendations before beginning the installation.

